+# CSV import
+
+## What and why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one. The import creates new contacts, updates
+existing ones, and returns a report of what happened so that people can fix the
+rejected rows and import again.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`, tags joined
+with `;`).
+
+## Interface (fixed by the owner — copied verbatim)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Behaviour
+
+### Reading the file
+
+- Bytes are decoded as UTF-8; a leading BOM is dropped. Text input that still starts
+  with the BOM character has it dropped too.
+- Comma is the only delimiter. Quoting follows the CSV rules that `export_csv` writes.
+- Header names are matched case-insensitively, ignoring surrounding spaces; column order
+  is free.
+- Recognised columns: `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, and the pair `first_name` +
+  `last_name` as a replacement for `name`. `email` is required. `name` is required, or
+  else both `first_name` and `last_name`. `phone` and `tags` are optional.
+- With `first_name`/`last_name`, the name is first and last joined with one space and
+  then validated like any name. Issues with it are reported with column `name`.
+- Other columns are ignored, with a warning (see below).
+
+### Row numbers and blank rows
+
+- `RowIssue.row` is the line number in the file. Every line counts, blank lines
+  included. The header is row 1 and the first contact is row 2 (when there are no
+  leading blank lines). A quoted cell spanning several lines is reported at the line
+  where its row starts.
+- A blank row is an empty line, or a row whose cells are all empty or spaces (Excel's
+  `,,,`). Blank rows are skipped silently: no report entry and no row limit count.
+- A contact row is any non-blank row after the header.
+
+### Whole-file rejection — `ImportFileError`, repository untouched
+
+- The file is empty or contains only blank rows (no header).
+- The header has no `email` column, or no `name` column and not both `first_name`
+  and `last_name`.
+- The bytes are not valid UTF-8. The message tells the user to re-save the file as
+  "CSV UTF-8".
+- There are more than 1000 contact rows. Blank rows don't count. Rows that would be
+  rejected do count.
+- *(My call; the owner didn't mind)* A recognised column appears twice in the header.
+- *(My call)* The CSV is malformed, e.g. a quote is never closed. The message names
+  the line.
+- *(My call)* A single cell is longer than the field limit of Python's `csv` reader,
+  131072 characters by default. If something else in the process raises that limit,
+  such a cell becomes an ordinary row error. Lifting that limit would change a process-wide setting, and such a
+  cell is almost certainly a broken file rather than a lead.
+
+A file with a header and no contact rows is valid and gives an empty report.
+
+### Row errors — row skipped entirely, nothing from it applied
+
+Each problem is one `RowIssue` in `errors`, with `column` set to the field. Every
+problem in the row is reported.
+
+- `name`: missing/blank, or invalid (e.g. too long), including on rows that update an
+  existing contact.
+- `email`: missing/blank or invalid.
+- `tags`: any tag in the cell is invalid.
+- `email`: the normalised email already appeared on an earlier contact row of the same
+  file. The first row wins; later rows are errors. *(My call)* This holds even when the
+  earlier row was itself rejected, so the outcome never depends on whether a row
+  before it was valid.
+- *(My call)* `column=None`: the row has more cells than the header has columns, even
+  if the extra cells are empty. Spreadsheets write rows as wide as the header, so an
+  extra cell means something shifted. The usual cause is an unquoted comma in a name. If such a row were imported,
+  its values would land in the wrong columns. Its cells are therefore not read at all:
+  this is the row's only error, and its email doesn't count as a use for the
+  repeated-email rule. Missing trailing cells are treated as empty.
+
+Rows with an error produce no warnings.
+
+### Applying a valid row
+
+The email is normalised, then looked up in the repository with the existing
+case-insensitive lookup.
+
+- **New contact** → added; its normalised email is appended to `created`.
+- **Existing contact** → updated, never duplicated; its normalised email is appended to
+  `updated` (also when nothing actually changed):
+  - name: the file's value replaces the stored one;
+  - phone: replaced only when the cell has a value (not empty or spaces). An empty
+    cell or a missing `phone` column keeps the stored phone;
+  - tags: the file's tags are added to the stored ones. An import never removes tags.
+- **Phone that fails validation** → not a row error. The row is applied without it:
+  the stored phone is kept, or a new contact gets no phone. A warning with column
+  `phone` is added for the row.
+- Tags cell: split on `;`, each tag normalised. Empty pieces (e.g. a trailing `;`) are
+  ignored.
+
+`created` and `updated` are in file order.
+
+### Warnings
+
+- `phone`: invalid phone, as above.
+- *(My call; the owner allowed warning or silence)* One warning per ignored named
+  header column, with `row` = the header row and `column=None`. This catches typos
+  like `phnoe`. Header cells with no name (Excel's trailing `,,`) are ignored
+  silently. If `name` is present, `first_name`/`last_name` are ignored too and get
+  this warning.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- [ ] A file in `export_csv` format, imported into an empty repository, recreates the
+      same contacts. `created` lists their emails in order.
+- [ ] Bytes with a UTF-8 BOM, and text starting with `﻿`, import the same as without it.
+- [ ] Non-UTF-8 bytes → `ImportFileError` mentioning CSV UTF-8; repo unchanged.
+- [ ] Empty file / blank-only file → `ImportFileError`.
+- [ ] Header-only file → empty report, no error.
+- [ ] Missing `email` column; missing `name` column (and no first/last pair) → `ImportFileError`.
+- [ ] `first_name` + `last_name` → name joined with one space. A blank pair → error with column `name`.
+- [ ] Headers match ignoring case and spaces, in any order. Unknown columns don't cause errors.
+- [ ] 1000 contact rows import. 1001 contact rows → `ImportFileError` and repo unchanged.
+      Blank rows don't count toward the limit.
+- [ ] Row numbers: header is 1, first contact is 2, and blank lines count.
+- [ ] Blank rows (empty line, `,,,`, cells of spaces) produce no report entries.
+- [ ] Invalid/missing name, invalid/missing email, invalid tag → error on that row with that column;
+      the row isn't applied, and an existing contact stays untouched.
+- [ ] Bad name on a row matching an existing contact → error; contact unchanged.
+- [ ] Repeated email in the file → later rows are errors with column `email`; first row applied.
+- [ ] Existing contact: name replaced, phone replaced only when the cell is non-empty, tags unioned;
+      email listed in `updated`, not `created`, and the repo size doesn't grow.
+- [ ] Empty phone cell or no phone column → existing phone kept.
+- [ ] Invalid phone → warning (column `phone`), rest of the row applied, existing phone kept / new
+      contact has no phone.
+- [ ] Unclosed quote → `ImportFileError`, repo unchanged.
+- [ ] `ImportFileError` is a `ContactError`.
+- [ ] `python3 -m pytest -q` exits 0.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Dry-run / preview mode.
+- Deleting contacts missing from the file.
+- A `ContactBook` method — `import_contacts` is the only entry point.
+- CSV-injection handling (an export concern).
+- Delimiters other than comma; encodings other than UTF-8.
+
+## Scope limits
+
+- New module `src/contactbook/importer.py`. `ImportFileError` is added to `errors.py`
+  and re-exported from the package. Existing modules are otherwise unchanged.
+- Standard library only.
